## Project State Summary

**Problem:** go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that regenerates classic Unix utilities in Go, verified via differential testing against GNU reference binaries. The goal is to stress-test the mage-claude-orchestrator code generation pipeline.

**Architecture:** A four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) produces Go binaries in cmd/ backed by shared libraries in pkg/ (testutils, sys, format). Mage targets orchestrate builds and generation. Differential testing in pkg/testutils compares Go output against Homebrew GNU binaries byte-for-byte.

**Current state:** 17 commands implemented across generation runs 1-17 (7,194 LOC total). All releases from rel00.0 through rel05.4 are marked "implemented" in the roadmap. The only remaining release with spec_complete status is rel99.2 (deferred ts utility). Code exists at generation-merged tags but is not on the working branch.

**Current release:** rel99.2 — contains one use case: rel99.2-uc001-ts (the moreutils `ts` timestamp-prepending utility). Status is spec_complete / code not started.

## Priority Analysis

rel99.2 is the only release in scope. It contains a single use case for `ts`. The PRD, use case, and test suite exist. The task is to generate the cmd/ts implementation. Since ts reads stdin and prepends timestamps, it depends on pkg/sys (for SIGPIPE handling) and pkg/testutils (for differential tests). Both are already implemented.

Per P2, implementation and tests must be separate tasks. With a max of 1 task, I propose the implementation task. Tests will follow in a subsequent session.

```yaml
- index: 0
  title: "cmd/ts implementation -- rel99.2-uc001-ts core timestamp prepending"
  dependency: -1
  description: |
    deliverable_type: code

    required_reading:
      - docs/specs/use-cases/rel99.2-uc001-ts.yaml (use case flow and success criteria)
      - docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel-99.2.yaml (test cases for acceptance)
      - docs/specs/product-requirements/ (find the ts PRD -- glob for prd*ts*.yaml)
      - docs/ARCHITECTURE.yaml (shared_protocols section -- differential testing pattern, SIGPIPE handling)
      - pkg/sys/ (InstallSIGPIPEHandler usage pattern)
      - pkg/testutils/ (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests for understanding test expectations)
      - cmd/tee/main.go (reference implementation of a stdin-processing utility for style consistency)
      - cmd/nl/main.go (reference implementation of a line-by-line stdin transformer for style consistency)

    files:
      - path: cmd/ts/main.go
        action: create
        note: "ts utility: reads stdin line by line, prepends formatted timestamps to each line"

    requirements:
      - id: R1
        text: |
          Implement the ts command entry point in cmd/ts/main.go. The program reads
          lines from stdin and writes each line to stdout with a timestamp prepended.
          The default timestamp format is the strftime-style format from the PRD
          (typically "%b %d %H:%M:%S"). Call pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() at the
          start of main per the shared SIGPIPE protocol.
      - id: R2
        text: |
          Support an optional positional argument for a custom strftime-compatible
          format string. Map strftime directives to Go time.Format layout strings.
          Cover at minimum: %Y, %m, %d, %H, %M, %S, %b, %T, %F, %s (epoch seconds),
          %N or %.s (sub-second precision if specified in the PRD). Refer to the PRD
          for the exact set of required directives.
      - id: R3
        text: |
          Implement the -s flag for relative (elapsed) timestamps. When -s is set,
          timestamps show the elapsed time since the program started rather than the
          wall clock time. The format for relative timestamps follows the PRD
          specification (typically HH:MM:SS or similar).
      - id: R4
        text: |
          Implement the -i flag for incremental timestamps. When -i is set,
          timestamps show the time elapsed since the previous line was output,
          not since program start. The -m flag (if specified in the PRD) selects
          a monotonic clock source for -s and -i modes. Handle flag interactions:
          -s and -i are mutually exclusive with the default absolute mode; -m
          modifies -s or -i behavior. Exit with code 0 on success, code matching
          the reference binary behavior on errors (e.g., invalid arguments).

    design_decisions:
      - id: D1
        text: |
          Use bufio.Scanner for line-by-line stdin reading, consistent with other
          cmd/ utilities in this project (nl, tac, fold). Write output with
          fmt.Fprintf to stdout.
      - id: D2
        text: |
          Map strftime format directives to Go time.Format layout by string
          replacement. Build the mapping table as a package-level var. Do not use
          an external strftime library -- the Go standard library time package is
          sufficient when combined with a directive-to-layout map.
      - id: D3
        text: |
          Use flag package for -s, -i, -m flags. Parse the optional format string
          from flag.Args() after flag.Parse(). Follow the flag parsing pattern used
          in cmd/tee and cmd/nl.
      - id: D4
        text: |
          For -s and -i modes, use time.Now() for wall clock and time.Since() for
          elapsed calculation. When -m is active, use a monotonic time source
          (Go's time.Now() already uses the monotonic clock for elapsed calculations
          via time.Since, so no special handling is needed beyond documenting it).

    acceptance_criteria:
      - id: AC1
        text: cmd/ts/main.go exists and compiles with "go build ./cmd/ts/"
      - id: AC2
        text: |
          Running "echo hello | go run ./cmd/ts/" produces a line with a timestamp
          prefix followed by "hello"
      - id: AC3
        text: |
          The -s flag produces relative timestamps starting near 00:00:00
      - id: AC4
        text: |
          The -i flag produces incremental timestamps showing inter-line delay
      - id: AC5
        text: |
          A custom format string argument (e.g., "%Y-%m-%d") changes the output format
      - id: AC6
        text: |
          pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() is called at the start of main()
      - id: AC7
        text: |
          "go vet ./cmd/ts/" reports no issues
```